WE haue declared in our former booke, how the nation of the Iewes  (after they had been vnder the subiection of the Macedonians) recouered  their liberty: we haue likewise discouered the great and grieuous conflicts, which Iudas their captaine performed, and in which he finally died in the behalfe of their libertie: how likewise (after the death of Iudas) all the wicked Apostate Iewes, who had reuolted from their religion, tooke courage, molesting and doing many iniuries to the rest of their countrimen. Finally, how besides their malice, the famine inuaded the countrey;

so that diuers being vnable to sustaine these two mortall scourges of famine and warre;  
